#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""superlooper — the William-facing CLI (plan Task 10, locked decision B.6).

  run             the foreground tick loop — always VISIBLE, in a cmux tab (it self-detects that
                  tab's pane; there is no launchd runner — a paneless daemon can't work, issue #33)
  stop            the deliberate OFF SWITCH (issue #239): record that this stop was the owner's
                  doing, hold the supervisor (the login-item job is booted OUT of gui/$UID, because
                  launchd's KeepAlive restarts even a clean exit), then stop the process by its
                  RECORDED pid. The marker it leaves is what the watchdog reads to decline
                  resurrecting the loop — without it a deliberate stop is the exact shape of a crash
  start           clear a deliberate stop and start the runner again. The login-item job is
                  bootstrapped back into gui/$UID; the pane home is handed its one-line manual
                  start, because a runner there lives in a tab a human opened. Any runner that boots
                  clears the stop marker too, so the off switch can never latch
  status          render the runner's own state (running / STOPPED BY OWNER / crashed) plus lanes /
                  gate / frozen / journal tail, from state + journal (no GitHub)
  adopt           write the config template into a target repo + seed CLAUDE.md standing rules +
                  create the §C.2 label set + print branch-protection recommendations
                  (print-only in v1)
  doctor          preflight: config, required_checks (FAILS HARD when empty), gh, labels,
                  accidental issue closes (COMPLETED with no merged PR behind it, issue #229),
                  cmux, jq, launch shim, activity hooks; --stack checks machine-level blocks
  morning-report  render reports/morning-<date>.md from the journal + notify (Task 11)
  nightly         fresh worktree of origin/<dev> -> qa.nightly_cmd -> flake/persistent triage;
                  a persistent failure freezes merges + files a scoped fix issue (Task 12)
  triage-flight   launch today's TRIAGE FLIGHT (`t<N>`, issue #449) — the queue-hygiene session
                  the owner delegated by standing rule. At most one a local day, only on a repo
                  that opted in, and only when some open unapproved issue changed since the last
                  verdicts. --check answers without consuming the day; --dry-run prints the brief
  triage-act      ONE verdict on ONE unapproved issue — the only write a flight may make. The
                  verdict selects the act (keep / fix / escalate / absorb a duplicate / close as
                  overtaken citing a commit really on origin/<dev> / close a nit AND file it in
                  the limitations ledger). It holds the delegation's edges at the call: an
                  approved or in-flight issue is escalated, never touched; a reopened issue with
                  an unchanged body is never re-closed; approval labels are refused
  triage-finish   close the run — every escalation composed into the owner's sitting sheet in the
                  run log, one line and one recommendation each, plus the run's tally
  promote-report  dev->prod EVIDENCE (suite vs known-failure ledger, merges since last promotion,
                  open issues) — NO verdict; William decides (Task 12)
  accept-failure  accept a failure by fingerprint into the ledger — one approval, ever (Task 12)
  request-restart ask the LIVE runner to restart ITSELF in its own cmux tab (issue #116): drops a
                  request marker the runner honors between ticks by re-exec'ing in place (fresh
                  engine, cleared in-memory state). Never launches/places a tab; with no live runner
                  it refuses and names the manual start. A local ops UI over the loop shells this
  tidy            close the session windows of FINISHED sessions (merged by default; --all extends
                  to parked/needs-william/bounced) — manual, never automatic; never touches an
                  in-flight or mid-gate lane, so it is safe to run while the runner is live. A
                  t<N> triage flight is a subject too (#463): one that closed its own run by
                  default, one whose session merely died under --all
  focus-session   bring ONE lane's session window to the front so you can watch it — and type into
                  it — with your own eyes (issue #339). Read-only about the FLEET: it never spawns,
                  sends, exits or kills, and it is not a viewer (the window it surfaces is the real
                  interactive terminal). Four outcomes, all reportable: focused / no_window (the
                  session ended or tidy closed it — an ordinary answer, not a failure) /
                  host_unreachable / unknown_lane. A local ops UI shells this instead of reaching
                  the session host itself
  janitor         propose GitHub-side cleanup (stale merged/superseded sl/* branches, open
                  `superseded` PRs, aged parked/needs-william issues, issues closed as
                  COMPLETED by a bare commit keyword with no merged PR behind them — issue #229 —
                  and open issues whose sl/i<N> PR already MERGED, which read as unstarted and can
                  be re-approved and run twice — issue #404)
                  and execute ONLY what William approves (y/N or --yes) — list/--dry-run changes
                  nothing anywhere; nothing is ever auto-closed, auto-deleted or auto-reopened
                  (issue #62, spec §8 V2)
  debug           launch ONE interactive sl-debugger session on this repo because a PERSON asked
                  (issue #144) — the attended sibling of `watchdog`'s unattended fallback. Owns the
                  d<N> id allocation and single-flight under the watchdog's own lock, composes the
                  brief from --note + --context-file, and journals the tap. A local ops UI over the
                  loop shells this instead of re-implementing the launch handshake. Once the spawn
                  CONFIRMS it brings that session's own window to the front (issue #459) — the tap
                  is attended, so the person who made it gets the terminal to watch and type into;
                  the window is best-effort and never the launch's verdict
  resume          re-enter an interrupted session's CONVERSATION instead of restarting it cold
                  (issue #298). Every launch mints a session id at spawn and records it in
                  state/sessions/<id>; this verb spends that record — `claude --resume <id>` back
                  into the same transcript, opening on a re-orientation preamble (branch, HEAD,
                  tree, PR — all re-read now), because a revived session remembers the conversation
                  and not the world. Refuses when the lane has no recorded id, when a session is
                  still live on it, or when its worktree is gone; --check is the read-only preflight
  upkeep          the WEEKLY once-over as one READ-ONLY command (issue #200): the machine stack,
                  installed-engine publish drift, the janitor's proposals, the ops-doc lint, the
                  branch/worktree census, the notify channel's last real delivery, and the week's
                  question/park rates — one short page. It executes nothing and writes nothing;
                  every finding names the existing owner-approved command that acts on it. There
                  is no --execute and there never will be (issue #200 Boundaries)
  watchdog        one MECHANICAL health check (issue #66): stale heartbeat / present ALERT /
                  the no-progress shape -> notify the owner -> grace window -> launch ONE
                  unattended sl-debugger session through the launch shim. Run it on an
                  interval via templates/launchd.watchdog.plist; state/WATCHDOG_OFF disables
"""
